// Crash/exhaustion backstop for the failure index (PR 1b; see
// docs/design/mirror-sync-failure-index.md "Crash/exhaustion backstop").
//
// The in-process path (consumer.recordTerminal) records a terminal failure
// just before it Terms the message. A worker that dies mid-handler never
// reaches that point, so the failure would be invisible — the only trace is
// the JetStream advisory nats-server emits when the message finally exhausts
// MaxDeliver (MAX_DELIVERIES) or is explicitly terminated (MSG_TERMINATED).
// This subscriber listens for those advisories, fetches the original message
// out of the stream by sequence, and runs the SAME RecordFailure CAS upsert.
//
// Idempotent against the in-process path: both key on the mirror_git_v1 stream
// sequence, and RecordFailure no-ops when seq <= LastSeq — so whichever path
// records a given failure second is a dedup no-op and the count isn't inflated.
//
// The two advisories differ in whether the message is still fetchable, because
// mirror_git_v1 is a WorkQueue stream (Ack OR Term REMOVES the message):
//   - MAX_DELIVERIES fires when a job exhausts MaxDeliver still UNacked — the
//     pod-crash gap. The message is therefore still in the stream, GetMsg
//     succeeds, and this is where genuine fresh captures (outcome=recorded) come
//     from.
//   - MSG_TERMINATED fires right after the worker Terms, which already removed
//     the message (and the in-process path recorded it just before Terming). So
//     GetMsg almost always MISSES here — outcome=missing is the expected, benign
//     steady state for this advisory, not a failure.
//
// GetMsg uses the request/reply STREAM.MSG.GET API (the stream isn't AllowDirect),
// which needs PUBLISH on $JS.API.STREAM.MSG.GET.mirror_git_v1 — covered by the
// mirror-worker user's $JS.API.> grant (fleet#973). A regression that removed it
// would surface loudly via advisoryPermErrorHandler, not as a silent miss.
//
// Scope: this recovers the POD-CRASH gap (the message was never acked/termed,
// so it's still in the stream). It does NOT recover a fail-open Term drop (a KV
// write that failed in-process because KV was down) beyond the incidental
// second chance a MSG_TERMINATED advisory gives if KV has since recovered —
// the accepted fail-open loss is owned by record_failed + the L2 reconcile.
//
// FLEET PREREQUISITE (already shipped in fleet#973): the mirror-worker NATS
// user must SUBSCRIBE-allow both advisory subjects below. Without the grant the
// broker rejects the subscription asynchronously and the channel simply never
