// merely untidy, they are load-bearing in the wrong direction: one
// mirror-pipeline pod emitted 57 ErrNoResponders inside 83 milliseconds as its
// in-flight fetches failed together during a deploy, and two services between
// them tripped a cluster's error-log alert three times in forty minutes. Every
// one of those alerts was accurate about the count and wrong about the
// meaning, which is how a signal stops being read (COR-1228). The same
// reasoning previously lived as hand-rolled ErrStreamNotFound warn-branches in
// each subscribe loop; those now route here too.
//
// The demotion is not unconditional, because "recoverable" is a claim about
// what happens next, not a property of the error value: the same
// ErrNoResponders means deploy churn when the next fetch succeeds and a down
// JetStream when nothing has succeeded for ten minutes. Each consumer loop
// holds a [Health] that its success path resets ([Health.Recovered]); a loop
// whose failures keep arriving with no recovery in between returns to error
// level after [escalateAfter], so a sustained outage still pages while
// absorbed churn — however long it goes on — stays quiet.
